I implemented option 1 for #1011. When a deny list is set, yoyo now refuses eval when its arguments contain a command substitution ($(...) or backticks). Build, clippy, fmt and the full test suite all pass, and the change is committed as 372a0d85.

What changed
• The check: eval_of_substitution in src/safety/indirect_command.rs runs only from user_deny_spelling_refusal, so only when a deny list exists. It reuses the file's existing segmenting and word lexer, so VAR= and export prefixes are already stripped. It also skips builtin/command in front of eval and looks inside bash -c '…' strings. Without that, builtin eval "$(…)" and bash -c 'eval "$(…)"' would have been easy ways around it.
• The refusal message says why the command can't be checked and gives the way out: run setup like eval "$(ssh-agent)" outside yoyo, or start the session without a deny list.
• Unchanged: users with no deny list see no difference, and a test pins that. The general analyser still lets eval "$(tool)" through as before. I updated its comment to point to the new check.
• Tests are in src/tools_user_deny_tests.rs, next to the existing user-deny tests:
  • The test that pinned this gap as a known limit now asserts the refusal instead, which is what its own comment asked whoever closed #1011 to do. I renamed it to eval_of_an_assembled_command_string_is_refused_with_a_deny_list.
  • Refused cases cover backticks, FOO=1 eval, export …; eval, cd x && eval $(…), builtin eval and bash -c.
  • Near-misses that must still pass: eval "echo hi", eval echo hi, echo "$(date)", evaluate "$(x)", echo eval "$(x)".
• Positive control: I disabled the new function, ran the tests and restored it in one command. Only the new refusal test failed; the near-miss and no-deny-list tests stayed green. Everything is green after the restore, with no leftover disable marker.
• Docs: I replaced the limitation sentence in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md with the new rule, what it costs (eval "$(ssh-agent)" is refused while a deny list is set) and the way out. It still says plainly that a deny list over spellings is not a sandbox. I also added a short dated note to ARCHITECTURE.md.

One choice to know about: any argument containing $( is refused, even inside single quotes, as in eval 'echo $(x)'. That refuses a bit more than strictly needed, and I chose it to keep the check simple. It's recorded in ARCHITECTURE.md.

The commit title says "Fixes #1011", so the push will close the issue. Before committing I checked for #1011 references in src, docs and skills: the only one was the old test comment, which is now rewritten, so nothing users see will point at the closed ticket.
